      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oil in a large saucepan over medium heat. Add the bacon and cook for 4 to 5 minutes until it starts to colour and release its fat."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the onion, leek, carrot, and celery. Cook, stirring occasionally, for 8 to 10 minutes until softened."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Rinse the split peas under cold water. Add them to the pot along with the stock, bay leaves, and thyme. Bring to a boil, skim off any foam, then reduce to a low simmer. Cook with the lid slightly ajar for 60 to 75 minutes, stirring every 15 minutes, until the peas have completely dissolved."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the bay leaves and thyme stalks. Stir in the mustard if using. Taste and adjust seasoning."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Ladle into bowls and scatter over the chopped parsley. Serve with dark rye bread."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Peas have not dissolved after 75 minutes"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Continue simmering and add more stock if the soup is too thick. Old dried peas can take up to 2 hours to fully soften."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Soup catches on the bottom"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use a heavy-based pan and stir frequently. Once the peas start to break down the soup gets thick and sticks easily; keep the heat low."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Soup tastes bland"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make sure the bacon coloured at the start. A teaspoon of Dijon mustard and a small splash of white wine vinegar stirred in at the end make a significant difference."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Erbsensuppe is one of the oldest soups in the German kitchen. It was the daily ration of the Prussian military and has remained a winter staple ever since. You will find it in canteens, farmhouse kitchens, and Christmas market stalls. It is the kind of soup that warms you through."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
